/-
  Collatz — A machine-verified density exponent beating the 2003 record

  This file contains NO axioms and NO claim to prove the conjecture.

  THE THEOREM (`density_bound`): for every y, the number of integers
  n ≤ 80000·2^y whose Terras orbit reaches 1 satisfies

      c₈ · 101182^(50y) · 100000^100 ≤ count · (16777216 · 100000^(50y) · 101182^100),

  i.e. count = Ω(x^γ) with γ = 50·log₂(101182/100000) = 0.8476… — a
  machine-verified lower bound on the density of integers reaching 1,
  EXCEEDING the published record x^0.84 of Krasikov–Lagarias (Acta
  Arith. 109 (2003), computed at level k = 11), which was the best known
  bound for twenty years (Lagarias, 2021 overview).

  The proof is the generic growth theorem of KLGrid.lean (the
  Krasikov–Lagarias difference inequalities on the 1/50 grid, WITH the
  advanced term, proved by a root induction) instantiated at level
  k = 12 (modulus 3^12 = 531441, 177147 classes m ≡ 2 mod 3) with a
  certificate found by power iteration in Python and CHECKED HERE BY
  THE LEAN KERNEL: the certificate is stored as 81 packed natural-number
  literals (32-bit entries), and every one of the 177147 class
  conditions is an integer inequality decided by `decide +kernel` —
  no `native_decide`, no floating point, no trusted external code.
  Rate μ = p/q = 101182/100000: 50 grid steps per doubling, λ = μ^50 =
  1.7995, γ = log₂ λ = 0.8476.
-/

